WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.820
This is a Wendover Productions video in collaboration
with RealLifeLore, and made possible by Squarespace.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:11.240
Build your website for 10% off by going to
squarespace.com/wendover.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:20.160
3,500 miles from Washington, D.C., 3,000 miles
north of Los Angeles, 750 miles north of Anchorage,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.680
where the ground never melts, where the snow falls
mid-summer, where no plants taller than a

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.040
few inches grow, where the sun sets for months
in the winter and stays up for months in the

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.270
summer, lies America’s northernmost town—Barrow,
Alaska.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:38.749
Combing through maps, Barrow has always intrigued
me and I had two questions—why does it exist

00:00:38.749 --> 00:00:41.140
and what is it like?—so I visited it.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.949
Now, asking why Barrow exists might seem like
an absurd question.

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:49.089
You could ask the same question for Fernley,
Nevada or Grafton, Vermont or any other town

00:00:49.089 --> 00:00:50.840
but Barrow is a bit unique.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.049
It’s not a small town, at least by Arctic
standards.

00:00:55.049 --> 00:00:59.440
Just as many people live in Barrow as in the
entire rest of Northern Alaska.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.449
There is only one town on earth larger than
and further north than Barrow (Tiksi, Russia)

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:09.079
Considering Barrow is one of the northernmost
communities in the world, the weather is far

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:10.140
from typical.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.119
This clip from 1:45 AM on May 30th shows just
how unique it is.

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:21.430
Just hours before, it was 68 degrees in Los
Angeles, 70 in Denver, 93 in D.C., and 96 in

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:25.759
Dallas while Americans across the country
were having their Memorial Day barbecues and

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:31.040
here in Barrow, the sun still lit up the landscape
and it billowed snow just before two in the

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:32.040
morning.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.509
Snowy weather isn’t in the least bit unique
in the weeks leading up to summer and even

00:01:35.509 --> 00:01:39.789
during June, July, and August, the ground
regularly gets a light dusting.

00:01:39.789 --> 00:01:41.630
You can tell it’s the Arctic.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:48.399
For 65 days in the summer, the sun never sets,
then for 65 again in the winter it never rises.

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:50.520
On the ground, Barrow is… desolate.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:56.360
It’s bounded on one side by the flat Arctic
tundra and the other by the often frozen Arctic Ocean.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.840
There’s
little emphasis on aesthetics within the town

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:03.009
itself but there are few sights as beautiful
as the sea-ice just off of the beach.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:08.000
There’s not a single paved road in Barrow
as they would be destroyed each year by the

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.830
shifting land as it freezes and melts.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:15.670
In fact, there aren’t even roads connecting
Barrow to the outside world—they end just

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.280
a couple miles out of town.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.100
That does mean,
though, that for all but a few months of the

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:24.340
year there’s only one way in or out—the
airplane.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:28.350
Barrow’s Airport is quite literally the
lifeline of the community.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:33.340
The entire town relies on planes going in
and out to get food in and people out.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:38.120
Somewhat surprisingly, multiple jet planes
fly into Barrow each day from Anchorage.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:42.980
You could start your day in this small town
above the Arctic Circle and finish in New York.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:48.600
With no roads or port, with little exception,
every ounce of food flies into Barrow.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:54.560
There’s even a special type of aircraft
built almost exclusively to serve small towns

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.590
like Barrow—the 737-combi.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:01.270
These planes have their front half built like
cargo planes and their back half built like

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:05.510
passenger planes to serve communities that
are small enough that they can’t demand

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.900
full-size cargo airplanes and still need a
way to bring passengers in and out.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.220
Increasingly nowadays, however, Barrow can
be supplied by boat.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:17.860
For just a few months of the year, the ice
breaks up enough that a barge can come to

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:22.080
shore and bring all the goods to Barrow that
won’t fit in a plane.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.620
That means that if you need a car or a truck
or building materials, you have one shot a

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:27.880
year to order it.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.220
As the mayor says:

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:34.980
[Mayor] "Cost of living is
very expensive here because a lot of the produce,

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.740
products are very expensive, because they’re
flown in.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.720
Air freight is very expensive.”

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:46.480
Heavy things are incredibly expensive—$15
for this hand sanitizer and $30 for this stain remover.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:52.480
Products that need refrigeration in transport,
such as frozen pizza, are also unnaturally

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.800
expensive—$17 for this one.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.590
Bulky things demand a premium too—$20 for
some toilet paper and $22 for diapers.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:07.160
The cost of living above the Arctic Circle
in Barrow is just extremely high due to its location.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:12.780
In visiting such a foreign place, you do sometimes
have to remind yourself that you’re still

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.880
in the same country as Miami, Minneapolis,
or Milwaukee.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.410
It’s a reminder of just how vast the United
States is.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:24.730
Barrow still has a US Post Office, a Wells
Fargo bank, those American-style signs—it’s

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:29.440
still an American city even if it’s closer
to Tokyo and St. Petersburg than it is to

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.440
D.C.

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.120
So why do people live in Barrow?

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.280
Why have 5,000 people chosen to live their
lives as close to the North Pole as to their

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.830
own state capital?

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:44.690
Well, for many residents, it’s been their
home for thousands of years.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:49.550
Barrow is the cultural center of the Iñupiat
tribe—one of the dozens of native Alaskan

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:50.550
tribes.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:54.610
There’s evidence that the Iñupiat people
have lived in the same spot as Barrow for

00:04:54.610 --> 00:05:00.410
more than 1,500 years making it one of the
oldest permanently inhabited settlements in

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:01.500
North America.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:06.090
That’s why over 60% of the residents of
the city are Alaskan Native—mostly from

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.400
the Iñupiat tribe.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:11.340
The answer to why the other 40% is there,
as it often is, is oil.

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:15.860
Barrow is the administrative center of the
North Slope borough—the equivalent of a

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.020
county or region.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:23.650
The area is larger than the entire United
Kingdom, yet less than 10,000 people live within

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:24.800
its borders.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.200
Barrow itself doesn’t have a significant
amount of oil, but nearby Prudhoe Bay has

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.100
the single largest oil deposit in North America.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.920
[Mayor] “The larger companies that have leases

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:45.880
out on the Prudhoe Bay oil fields hire people
to fill in positions that are needed within

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:47.110
their services.”

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:51.340
Some people go from Barrow to Prudhoe Bay
to work the drills, but there’s also good

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:55.240
work within Barrow as it serves as the home
of the borough’s government.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:00.030
The primary employers in the area are the
city, borough, state, and federal government.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.889
And, in fact, you can make some decent money
in Barrow.

00:06:03.889 --> 00:06:10.700
The median household income is over $80,000
compared the US’ average of about $55,000.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:15.300
Of course, when spending
$500 a week on groceries is normal, this number

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:19.700
seems slightly less impressive, but the city
does have real industry.

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:23.570
That, of course, is a big reason why 5,000
people live here.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:26.100
It’s a completely self-sustaining city.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:31.120
It’s expensive, hugely expensive, to live
here but those who do make that choice for

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.120
a reason.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:35.980
Among American cities, Barrow sits center
stage watching the world warm.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:39.570
-“I think we live through climate change
on a daily basis.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:44.919
Climate change and global climate change has
its effects on the North Slope and some see

00:06:44.919 --> 00:06:52.770
different effects over time as it occurs depending
on the season.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:07:01.880
Mostly, you can see it in the fall and
spring seasons in terms of the effects of climate

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:09.800
change—the thawing, the warmer temperatures,
movements of and migrations of animals occurring

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:15.030
a little bit more earlier than what they were
in the past.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.620
These are changes we’re contending with
on a daily basis in the Arctic.”

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:26.310
Day by day, the oil supplies in the North Slope
Borough run out and the world is turning to

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:27.830
renewable energy.

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:31.870
The area has a looming threat of its biggest
industry moving out.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:37.620
Many other places in a similar situation such
as the UAE or Oman or Norway have attempted

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:41.590
to fill the gap that oil will leave by growing
the tourism industry.

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:45.919
But, as Mayor Brower says, “It’s very seasonal.

00:07:45.919 --> 00:07:51.900
It's only from the spring to the fall—the
majority of the tourist season that occurs.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:58.790
There’s some interest in developing tourist
entrepreneurship into the communities but

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:05.889
the infrastructure still lacking—a lack
of hotels, a lack of transportation needs

00:08:05.889 --> 00:08:11.180
or tourism business items that are needed
to conduct tourism, they’re not quite developed

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:13.460
in a lot of our villages.”

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:18.910
Visiting Barrow is an adventure, not a vacation,
and nobody should come expecting a McDonald's,

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.610
and Segway tours, and super-fast Wi-Fi.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:26.240
The beach of America’s northernmost town
presents one of the rarest views in the world—thousands

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:31.360
of miles of civilization-free ice, water,
and nothingness.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.389
Alaska’s license plates may say that the
state itself is America’s last frontier,

00:08:36.389 --> 00:08:40.960
but nowhere feels closer to the end of the
Earth than Barrow, Alaska.

